tree-sitter-markdown: serialize only the open blocks that fit

The block scanner serializes five bytes of state and then four bytes for
every open block. A block quote, list item or other container nested 255
deep takes 1025 of the 1024 bytes tree-sitter gives a scanner's state: a
line of 255 '>' is enough. With its assertions on, tree-sitter's parser
aborts the process on the length the scanner returns; without them, the
bytes past the buffer are overwritten.

serialize writes as many blocks as fit, 254, and leaves out those nested
deeper, so the scanner restores 254 open blocks and deeper nesting parses
as an error, as the bash, Ruby and HTML scanners do with state that does
not fit. Within 254 blocks, the one-byte count of the blocks a line has
matched stays in range.

Applies to tree-sitter-markdown/src/scanner.c of tree-sitter-markdown at
commit 2dfd57f547f06ca5631a80f601e129d73fc8e9f0.

diff --git a/tree-sitter-markdown/src/scanner.c b/tree-sitter-markdown/src/scanner.c
index c7103cba256f615476dd95e216b69c0b21a7b542..c34c150a8582d8c38f8bf9292471ad6443ff9a25 100644
--- a/tree-sitter-markdown/src/scanner.c
+++ b/tree-sitter-markdown/src/scanner.c
@@ -244,6 +244,14 @@ static unsigned serialize(Scanner *s, char *buffer) {
     buffer[size++] = (char)s->column;
     buffer[size++] = (char)s->fenced_code_block_delimiter_length;
     size_t blocks_count = s->open_blocks.size;
+    // The state has TREE_SITTER_SERIALIZATION_BUFFER_SIZE bytes. Blocks
+    // nested deeper than fit are left out, so the parse of absurdly deep
+    // nesting degrades instead of writing past the end of the buffer.
+    size_t max_blocks =
+        (TREE_SITTER_SERIALIZATION_BUFFER_SIZE - size) / sizeof(Block);
+    if (blocks_count > max_blocks) {
+        blocks_count = max_blocks;
+    }
     if (blocks_count > 0) {
         memcpy(&buffer[size], s->open_blocks.items,
                blocks_count * sizeof(Block));
